WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.892 --> 00:00:04.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.540
HADLEY WICKHAM: So today
I am excited to talk

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.030
about the third
edition of testthat.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:13.650
So I'm going to start by talking
a little bit about like what

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.552
do I mean by that.

00:00:15.552 --> 00:00:17.760
You certainly all know what
a version of a package is

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.500
but what do I mean by this being
the third edition of testthat.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.500
And I'm going to talk through
kind of three big new features

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.230
in this version,
in this edition,

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:33.450
see how testthat now displays
differences much more clearly

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.040
using the Waldo package.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.190
We'll talk about a new type of
testing, new type of testing

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.260
to test that snapshot tests.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.380
Very briefly talk about testing
in parallel or multi process

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:44.143
testing.

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:46.560
And then finally, I'm going
to give you a real quick goody

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.990
bag of a bunch of
little small stuff

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.810
that will hopefully make your
life a little bit easier when

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.230
using testthat.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.700
So the big news
with this release

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.970
of testthat this upcoming
release of testthat 3.0

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.770
is we're going to introduce
the idea of this third edition.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.550
And I should say
anything you're going

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.650
to hear about today
is also explained

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.903
in more detail in vignettes
on the testthat website.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.570
If you want to learn
more about anything

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:16.080
I say today feel free to take
a look at those vignettes

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.842
and if they don't
explain, they don't

00:01:18.842 --> 00:01:20.300
help you understand
what's going on

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.310
please file an issue so
they can [INAUDIBLE] better.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.040
So the basic idea
of the edition is

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.440
that we want to make
breaking changes to testthat.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.680
Testthat is like 10
years old or something

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.600
and there's a bunch of things in
the package that we now regret.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.570
But testthat is kind of a
victim of its own popularity.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.070
There's something like 4,000
or 5,000 packages on CRAN

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.335
that use test that
and many of them

00:01:46.335 --> 00:01:47.460
are not actively developed.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.320
So there's no way
we can change test

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.440
that without breaking
a bunch of packages,

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.680
and many of these packages since
they wouldn't be maintained

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.380
people would just end up off
CRAN because people wouldn't

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.620
have the energy to fix them.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.730
So the idea of an
edition is that this

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.130
is something you have to
deliberately opt into.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.387
So if you want to use
many of the new features

00:02:10.387 --> 00:02:11.970
or all of the new
features of testthat

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.360
that I talk about today you
have to deliberately choose

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.060
to use the third
edition of testthat.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.560
And to sort of do
that you basically

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.583
have to add a line description
file, config/testthat/edition.

00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:26.250
So if you don't do
this, you'll continue

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.500
to use the second edition,
that's the existing

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.880
behavior of testthat.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.950
If you do change this you'll
get a bunch of new features

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.500
and a bunch of things
which I'll explain shortly

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.920
which have gone away.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:45.190
So the kind of idea is that
it should take hopefully

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.590
less than 30 minutes
to convert a package

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.090
to use the third edition.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:52.280
It's a little bit of work,
hopefully, not too much work.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.070
But it just
generally gets you up

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.580
to date with all about
current best practices.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.170
And I should say
this kind of idea

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.720
or this edition idea
is successful it's

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.130
something we're
likely to try out

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:06.610
in other packages as a way
of allowing existing code

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:10.420
to continue working while
giving you the choice to opt

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:13.915
into a new simplified edition.

00:03:13.915 --> 00:03:17.300
So editions will always be
coupled with a major version,

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:21.260
so that means testthat 2 and
everything else before it

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:22.760
counts as the second edition.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.800
Testthat 3.0 can use the second
edition as the default behavior

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:30.290
or you can opt into
using the third edition

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:33.680
and if one day many probably
multiple years down the line

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.020
we decide to introduce
a fourth edition then

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:40.390
that would be tied
to testthat 4.0.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:42.633
So what's going away
in the third edition.

00:03:42.633 --> 00:03:44.050
These are the
things that are most

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.620
likely to lead you to change
a test to other context.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.210
Function is going
away, we've kind

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:53.740
of being moving away from this
for quite some time in favor

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.863
of just using the
name of the text file.

00:03:55.863 --> 00:03:58.030
There's no need to kind of
duplicate the information

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.220
in the test file in
another function which

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.870
you have to remember to update
if you ever loose your test

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.030
files.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.660
We've also been lately in
devtools and users building

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:11.860
kind of a strong coupling
between files in our directory

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:13.330
and files in the
test directories

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.160
so that there is a
kind of a one to one

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.519
correspondence which gives
you a bunch of handy keyboard

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:21.610
shortcuts and development tools.

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.400
The very old expect that
function is going away.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.840
This was the kind of first API.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.600
The first excessively
clever API that testthat

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.150
use with every test
kind of had the flavor.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.570
You could read it like a
sentence like testthat law

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:41.320
is true and I pretty
quickly decided

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.340
that that was excessively
clever not very good,

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.820
and so we're finally
deprecating that for good.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:55.360
Expect is we are deprecating in
favor of expect is three class,

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.700
expect is four class
and expect type of just

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.620
to be more precise
about what exactly

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.390
you're trying to express.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.670
Expect equivalent, I'll talk
about a little bit later.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.753
But we're basically that's
just giving an argument

00:05:05.753 --> 00:05:08.410
to expect equal or
expect identical.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:11.260
Some of the mocking
functions going away

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.080
in favor of more
feature packages.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.450
And then finally, we are
moving away from setup

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:20.470
and tear down in favor
of test fixtures,

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:22.675
which I'm not going
to talk about today.

00:05:22.675 --> 00:05:24.300
But there's a whole
vignette about them

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.910
if you want to learn more.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.670
And hopefully, that's kind
of all, I need to say,

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.310
just these are the functions
that are going away.

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.973
Like if you really, really
believe any of these functions

00:05:34.973 --> 00:05:36.390
are important,
like now's the time

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:39.420
to speak up and say you
want to keep these otherwise

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.610
when you switch the second--

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:43.920
search of the third edition
re-run on your tests

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.038
you'll get deprecation
messages telling you what

00:05:46.038 --> 00:05:47.246
to replace instructions with.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.310
So that's kind of the
stuff that's going away now

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:56.220
I want to focus on
the cool new features.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.790
And I think the feature you're
most likely to encounter

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.520
and the most likely to give
you pleasure in your life

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.730
is the comparisons with
expect equal and expect

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:09.390
identical with test
failures with expect equal

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:15.460
and expect identical and now
much easier to understand.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.080
So previously expect
equal uses the base

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:19.350
all dot equal function.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:20.910
This was never
really what all dot

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:24.570
equal was intended for but
it kind of did an OK job.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.330
But there's a few cases
where it just doesn't

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:28.750
give very useful output.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:31.740
And so here I am
comparing empty cars

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:35.140
without the first
column to empty cars

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:36.930
but the full data set.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:40.120
And you'll notice I
get a lot of output.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.645
Well it tells me that there's
10 string mismatches which

00:06:42.645 --> 00:06:44.850
is in the names that
doesn't really help me.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.353
It tells me there's
a length mismatch

00:06:47.353 --> 00:06:49.770
and then it gives me a bunch
of mean relative differences.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:52.500
So this certainly tells me
that there's a difference

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.993
but it doesn't kind
of concisely crisply

00:06:55.993 --> 00:06:58.410
point me to the fact there's
a column missing in this data

00:06:58.410 --> 00:06:59.460
frame.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.600
So now we use Waldo.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:03.463
So to use this--

00:07:03.463 --> 00:07:05.880
I'm just going to use this
function called local addition.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.350
This is not something
you generally

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:10.140
need to use in your
tests because you'll

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:13.260
convert a whole package
to use the third edition

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.600
but we provide this
local edition function

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.220
for writing stuff like
this, for writing vignettes.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:21.510
So you can explain
about this new edition.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:25.500
This just temporarily sets the
edition to the third edition.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.730
So now we use the third
edition and now this

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:31.380
is the new Waldo output.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.480
So the kind of the
first principle

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.120
is that Waldo is going
to give you the most

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.270
important differences
first and in this case

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:40.980
I think the most
important difference is

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.170
that the length are different.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.740
And then it tells us
what the names are,

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:53.060
how the names have changed
lining up the old names--

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:56.930
lining up sorry, the actual
names or the expected names

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.850
and then it uses
color to highlight

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:03.380
the fact that in the expected is
another column called the mpg.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:05.918
And then finally, down here,
we get an exact description.

00:08:05.918 --> 00:08:07.710
Well, the names are
different but the value

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:09.350
are also different.

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:16.410
The actual that's-- and as I
read this that is clearly not

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:19.770
the case because it's absent
from the expected so I think

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.138
I've messed up the labels here.

00:08:21.138 --> 00:08:22.680
So I'll have to fix
these they should

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:25.810
say expected mpg is
absent and this should

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:28.990
say actual mpg is a double.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:33.690
So that just allows you once I
fix those labels to precisely

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.970
see what the difference is.

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:39.360
So here I have switched
to use the third edition,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.330
Waldo then shows the most
important differences first.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:46.170
It uses color to help
highlight differences.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:49.260
Oh, and always use the names
of things where possible just

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.900
because that makes it
easier to understand exactly

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:54.540
where the differences lie.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:57.060
So another little
example here I had

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:03.330
a factor versus an ordered
factor with one extra level.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:05.460
When you look at the
expect equal output

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:09.360
from the second edition you'll
see like it kind of tells you

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:11.070
that they're different
but it's hard

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.230
because it doesn't
include the values to know

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:15.342
exactly what's changed.

00:09:15.342 --> 00:09:16.800
When we switch to
the third edition

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.450
now we can say there's a new
element of the class that

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.500
gets ordered and there's a new
element of the levels, which

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.370
is d and hopefully this will
make it much, much easier

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.770
to see exactly what's going on.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:30.510
And certainly in my own--

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:33.120
this is the number one reason
I've been converting packages

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.430
to use the third edition to
test that just because it

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:39.490
makes things so
much easier to study

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.762
when something's gone wrong.

00:09:41.762 --> 00:09:43.470
So if you want to
learn more about Waldo,

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.480
you can go to the
Waldo websites which

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:48.340
shows a bunch of
kind of examples,

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:51.580
shows some of the principles.

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:53.290
A lot of the differences
of the values

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:57.580
are powered by this really
nice package called diffobj

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:02.530
by Brodie Gaslam, which uses
the same algorithm as the diff

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:06.190
utility on Linux which just
makes it really easy to narrow

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:08.440
and exactly what has changed
between two packages.

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.150
This change also
makes it now possible

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:17.150
to be precise about what's
the difference between expect

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:19.040
equal, expect
identical, and expect

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.610
equivalent, which are always
a little vague in the past.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:25.730
Now all of these functions
are equivalent to expect

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:29.370
identical but with some
extra arguments here.

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:31.280
So if you use
expect equal that's

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:34.250
equivalent to expect
identical, except that it

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:37.970
ignores small floating
point differences.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:41.390
Or if you use expect equivalent,
which is now deprecated

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:43.490
it's just the same
as using expect equal

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:46.790
or expect equivalent with
ignore attribute equals true.

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:49.160
That's the only thing that
expect equivalent does

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:50.533
is it just ignores attributes.

00:10:50.533 --> 00:10:52.700
Now we're going to make
that precise in the function

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:53.840
argument.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:57.230
And then any other
arguments and identical

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:00.290
expect equal are passed onto
Waldo compare which gives you

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:06.523
the ability to fine tune
your comparisons as needed.

00:11:06.523 --> 00:11:07.940
Now, this is such
a great idea you

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:10.850
might wonder why kind I
work with the second edition

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:11.690
as well.

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:14.820
Well, unfortunately when
I implemented expect equal

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:20.310
it turns out that I made
a rather silly mistake

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:23.540
when implementing the
tolerance comparison.

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:26.750
So depending on exactly which
code path, it goes down,

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:30.000
it computes the tolerance
in slightly different ways.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.020
One where it always uses
the absolute tolerance,

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.570
and one where it uses absolute
or relative tolerance.

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:38.450
There's basically no way,
I did a few experiments,

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:40.460
there's no way
regardless of which one

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:43.640
I pick it causes like hundreds
of print packages to break,

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:46.550
so that's the main
reason that this

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:48.950
has to be in the third edition.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:50.450
But there's a few
other small things

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:54.290
particularly if you are testing
code in a modeling package,

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:55.890
all dot equal
which used to power

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:58.550
expect equal ignores
environments of formulas

00:11:58.550 --> 00:11:59.930
and models--

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:04.070
sorry, formulas and functions.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:08.150
Waldo does not but it
provides a couple of arguments

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:12.480
to allow you to override
that if you need to.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.250
And there's a few other small
things around like [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:19.430
So I think the behavior of
Waldo is generally better,

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.470
it's a little bit
stricter and when it fails

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:23.150
it'll give you clearer results.

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:24.980
But we can't just turn
it on for everyone

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:26.660
because it would break
a bunch of tests.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:30.630
So you're going to have to
deliberately opt in to it.

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:35.190
So that's Waldo I think that's
like kind of number 1 feature.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:38.530
Likes again I have
found it so useful

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:42.150
when debugging tests myself
to have that much much, much

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.640
clearer display.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.473
The next big feature I want to
talk about is snapshot testing.

00:12:48.473 --> 00:12:49.890
Again, this comes
with a vignette,

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:51.473
so if you want to
learn more about it,

00:12:51.473 --> 00:12:52.560
you can read the vignette.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.030
If the vignette
doesn't make sense,

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:57.720
please file an issue so
we can make it better.

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:03.190
But the basic idea is this
is a new type of testing.

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:08.620
So normally in unit tests you
describe the expected output

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:09.970
using codes.

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:12.160
And in the vast
majority of cases

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:13.810
this is a really,
really good idea

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:15.610
because it allows
you to describe

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:17.890
what you expect
and in some scenes

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.740
the tests help
documents, because it

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:24.650
tests the code and code is
a means of communication.

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.160
They kind of help describe
the expected behavior

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:29.080
of the functions as well.

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.450
But sometimes describing
the expected output

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:32.950
is just like really annoying.

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:35.380
Like if it contains a bunch
of spacial characters,

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:39.160
like quotes or back slashes you
have to spend a bunch of time

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:40.982
like carefully escaping them.

00:13:40.982 --> 00:13:42.440
And then when
something goes wrong,

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:45.320
it's hard to see exactly,
like you've got to escape

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:49.090
and when you can it's just a
pain or maybe it's very large.

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:51.430
Like maybe you want to
check an entire HTML

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:55.870
page or multiple paragraphs
of text as you expect

00:13:55.870 --> 00:13:58.600
or maybe it's not even
something that you can usually

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:03.540
describe with text it's an
image or something else.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:08.950
So the idea of a snapshot
a golden tests which idea

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:12.830
used in other programming
languages, and other testing

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:20.110
packages, and test
that inspiration

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:23.800
draws primarily from Jest
which is a JavaScript testing

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:28.390
package that Joe Chang shared
his experiences with me a bunch

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:32.290
and persuaded me that this
is something really useful.

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:35.050
But so the snapshot
test the key idea

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:37.690
is that instead of recording
the results in line

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:40.750
and the test itself that's
stored in a separate file.

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:45.990
And test that provides a bunch
of tools for looking after that

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:47.610
for managing that file.

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:50.287
It created automatically
the first time you run it

00:14:50.287 --> 00:14:52.620
and then it gives you tools
to update it when you decide

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:55.270
it really has good change.

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:58.470
And if you've used verify
output or expect known output

00:14:58.470 --> 00:14:59.970
which we've never
really advertised

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:02.262
because we've never really
been particularly happy with

00:15:02.262 --> 00:15:07.740
and the snapshot tests basically
supersede those functions.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:10.963
So what does a snapshot
test looks like.

00:15:10.963 --> 00:15:12.380
So here I'm just
going to give you

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:15.440
a quick simulation
in the presentation

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:18.320
and I'll show you what this
looks like in a real package.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:20.343
So here I've got
two files foo dot

00:15:20.343 --> 00:15:24.530
R I've just got this very
silly and simple function which

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:26.570
has a mistake in it,
which you can see,

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:28.450
which we'll fix shortly.

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:30.470
And then I've got a test.

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:35.420
So I run foo, I expect
it as a character vector,

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:38.960
and then I'm going to use this
new expectation expect snapshot

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:40.120
output.

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:43.190
And this doesn't have like
what the expected value is

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:46.050
because it's going
to save to disc.

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:49.090
So the first time I run this
it's going to say warning

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:52.870
create snapshot reference
and it creates a new file,

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:58.285
and I forgot to put the create
directory in here inside the--

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:03.900
inside the test dot directory is
going to create a new directory

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:08.790
called snaps, _snaps, and inside
that it's going to have a file

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:10.800
called foo dot md.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.970
So R file is called foo dot R.
Our test file is called test

00:16:14.970 --> 00:16:17.470
dash food dot R. And
then our snapshot

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:21.790
for that, the snapshot file is
going to be called foo dot md.

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:25.500
So all of the users names have
a strong naming conventions

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:27.990
so you can easily
find which snapshot

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:31.932
corresponds to which test, which
corresponds to which R file.

00:16:31.932 --> 00:16:33.390
So what does that
snapshot contain?

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.490
Well, it's a markdown file.

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:37.210
Kind of explain
the syntax shortly.

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:40.770
But we use a heading to
indicate that this is the test

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:46.210
and then it's going to put
the output of that test

00:16:46.210 --> 00:16:48.800
directly in that file.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:52.368
So now if I run it again,
if I run that test again,

00:16:52.368 --> 00:16:54.535
the test is going to work
because nothing's changed.

00:16:54.535 --> 00:16:58.260
And so the test will pass.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:03.290
If I change it to fix that
typo, I run the test again,

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:06.630
I'm going to get an era saying
that the previous value was

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:09.020
something complicated
and the new value

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:11.099
is something complicated.

00:17:11.099 --> 00:17:13.980
Now, the downside
of snapshot tests

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:16.980
is that there's no way
to know what is correct.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:20.530
So you as a human now have
to step in and intervene.

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:22.230
So if this is
correct if you really

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:26.970
did mean to make this change
you can run snapshot accept

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:30.310
and that will accept the change.

00:17:30.310 --> 00:17:32.200
And the way that this
works is that when

00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:36.160
you have this value, when
something has changed

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:38.020
there will be a
new markdown file

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:41.350
which contains the new
value if you accept it then

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:44.740
it will replace that with
the [INAUDIBLE] value.

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:48.940
So let's go to the big picture.

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:53.400
So let's just dive into
that for a slightly more

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:54.180
realistic example.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:55.540
Start with a simple one.

00:17:55.540 --> 00:17:58.890
So I have this package,
I have the same test

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:02.250
which I copied before and
I'm going to run this test.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:06.390
And I'm going to run this test
by pressing command t which

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:09.390
is a shortcut but div tools.

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:10.860
Test file.

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:15.520
So this keyboard shortcut takes
advantage of that convention

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:18.090
that if you've got a
file called foo dot R

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:20.175
the corresponding test
will be test dot foo.

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:28.170
And so this says I've
added a new snapshot.

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:30.330
This is the value of
that snapshot I can

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:32.610
look at it as a markdown file.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:34.630
Again, which I'll
explain shortly.

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:39.930
And then if I run
that test again

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:43.140
you'll see that all
the test have passed.

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:45.660
The other thing if you've used
dev tool test file before--

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:47.340
which I'll talk
about briefly later--

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.130
is there's now a slightly
different display,

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:52.890
slightly more compact display
that hopefully make it easier

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:57.640
to run tests for single
file interactively.

00:18:57.640 --> 00:19:02.220
So if I change this, I'm
going to correct that typo.

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:05.740
I'm going to test
again and now the test

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:09.260
files because a
snapshot has changed.

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:11.240
So it shows you
the current value,

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:13.060
which is something
complicated and it

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:16.910
shows you the previous value
which is something complicated.

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:19.700
Now, if this is a
deliberate change

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:21.860
you can run snapshot accept.

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:25.610
If this wasn't a deliberate
change or that was just a typo,

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:30.050
I can fix it and rerun and
all my tests pass again.

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:31.693
So if you've ever
used verify output.

00:19:31.693 --> 00:19:33.860
This is a little bit different
because verify output

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:39.050
automatically update with a kind
of true known value [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:41.900
forced you to use it
with get, basically.

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:45.830
So I'm going to fix
this, the test file.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:49.370
You can see now in my
snaps directory I got foo.

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:52.490
That's the foo dot md
that's the previous value,

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:55.790
and I've got foo dot new dot
md, which is the new value.

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:01.922
So I look at this I decide that
it actually correct and then I

00:20:01.922 --> 00:20:03.130
can just copy and paste this.

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:16.610
And now foo dot md
is the correct result

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:18.620
and I can test that again.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:23.700
OK.

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:26.920
So let's look at a slightly
more complicated example.

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:31.440
So this is a bullets
function and it's basically

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.775
used to create HTML bullets.

00:20:43.210 --> 00:20:45.540
So it looks like this.

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:47.180
And this is like
a little annoying

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:50.780
to test because if you're going
to put this in a test you'd

00:20:50.780 --> 00:20:53.960
have to escape all of these
new lines and kind of carefully

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:56.420
manage all the white
space, it's just a pain.

00:20:56.420 --> 00:20:59.390
So to test this code
it's a little easy

00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:02.370
to use a snapshot test.

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:07.800
So I can run this
test and now here

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:13.770
is a test snapshot file with
multiple expectations in it.

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:16.170
The first one I am
just using a bullet

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:21.380
with a single bullet and the
second also is a single bullet

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:23.650
and also is the ID.

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:25.980
So when later I want to
change this bullets function,

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:29.145
maybe I've decided actually
I don't want this indent.

00:21:29.145 --> 00:21:31.520
Let's get rid of that.

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:34.230
I rerun the test.

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:38.040
Again, the changes use Waldo
to highlight what's changed.

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:41.290
So it's a little bit
hard to see this,

00:21:41.290 --> 00:21:44.670
but this is the thing that's
the same it's in gray.

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:47.130
What's changed while
we can see there

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:53.455
is now no space where
there was before.

00:21:56.380 --> 00:21:59.880
So I look at this I decide,
yep, that's a deliberate change,

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:03.860
and then I run snapshot
accept bullets.

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:05.570
Bullets to update that snapshot.

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:16.140
And that basically is snapshot.

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:17.980
Of course, there's
more documentation.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:20.680
And I think it'll take a little
while to get you hit around it

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:24.190
or I've shown you expect
snapshot output here,

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:27.490
just also expect snapshot, which
as Waldo's printed output also

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:30.790
captures messages,
warnings, errors.

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:32.560
There's also expect
snapshot error

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:34.360
if you want to
capture specifically

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:36.445
just error messages
and then we've

00:22:36.445 --> 00:22:39.700
got expect snapshot value
which captures return value.

00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:41.580
This is a little bit different.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:43.600
This is if you for
example, wanted

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.858
to test the output from
a complicated function,

00:22:46.858 --> 00:22:48.400
you just want to
make sure it doesn't

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:51.240
change without warning you
can use expect snapshot value.

00:22:54.153 --> 00:22:55.570
And then again,
of course, there's

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:57.750
the vignette which
you can look at.

00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:08.880
I guess I can't show
it because I did not--

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:10.520
I've installed the development
vision of the package which

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:13.100
doesn't have the vignettes
but you can go to the website.

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:14.870
Actually, let's just
go to the website.

00:23:21.323 --> 00:23:22.990
And then we can see
all these vignettes.

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:27.740
Here's one on
snapshot testing just

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:29.988
giving you a little bit more
detail about what I just

00:23:29.988 --> 00:23:30.530
talked about.

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:36.950
The only other thing
to mention is just

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:39.900
what are these files look like
why are they markdown files?

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:42.288
Well, if you're
writing this package

00:23:42.288 --> 00:23:43.830
as a collaborative
project and you're

00:23:43.830 --> 00:23:48.140
using GitHub or some other
tool where you do code review

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:51.680
it's really important that these
snapshots be human readable.

00:23:51.680 --> 00:23:53.720
Because when someone
goes to review your code

00:23:53.720 --> 00:23:55.680
they need to go look at
the snapshot and say,

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:57.840
is this a reasonable
change or not.

00:23:57.840 --> 00:23:59.990
So they use markdown.

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:04.130
Each file again there's one
snapshot file for the test file

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:08.700
which only corresponds to
a file in the directory.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:11.090
There's a heading
for the test name.

00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:13.690
And then if you have multiple
snapshot expectations

00:24:13.690 --> 00:24:15.170
of a single test
that is separated

00:24:15.170 --> 00:24:16.970
by the horizontal
roll and three dashes.

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:24.390
One thing that I'm working on
at the moment and with some help

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:28.220
from Joshua Canst
is a shiny app that

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:30.740
will help you review
all of these differences

00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:34.580
and accept them or reject them
by clicking buttons rather

00:24:34.580 --> 00:24:36.230
than typing stuff
at the console,

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:39.290
and the other big part of that
is providing tools for doing

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:41.340
image snapshots as well.

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:45.080
So this is something that
we've implemented in two places

00:24:45.080 --> 00:24:47.810
and shiny test, which is
used for testing shiny,

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:51.080
and vdiffr, which is
used for testing ggplot2.

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:54.230
The idea is they're going
to pull out the common code,

00:24:54.230 --> 00:24:56.150
centralize and test
that and invest

00:24:56.150 --> 00:24:58.430
a bunch in this
whole snapshot idea.

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:00.140
So you've got a
really nice workflow

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:02.720
if you do need to do image test.

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:04.430
Image tests are
complicated because they

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:08.000
can change for all sorts of
reasons unrelated to your code

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:09.890
but sometimes
they're all you have

00:25:09.890 --> 00:25:11.630
and they are really
important part

00:25:11.630 --> 00:25:14.480
of testing both shiny
itself and ggplot2 itself.

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:18.920
So that's snapshot tests.

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:23.530
Main idea of snapshot test is
compared to regular unit tests

00:25:23.530 --> 00:25:26.260
which have the expected
results in the test file,

00:25:26.260 --> 00:25:29.710
snapshot tests store the
expected results in another

00:25:29.710 --> 00:25:35.140
file, which makes them suitable
for testing large output,

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:39.400
output with quote marks and
backslash in it and for testing

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:44.910
things that you simply cannot
describe with text like images.

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:48.990
Next, I wanted to talk
briefly about parallel tests.

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:53.130
This is still a work in
progress by [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:55.650
And again, you have to
activate this specifically

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:56.430
for your package.

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:59.910
Again, by putting another
line in your description

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:02.280
config testthat parallel true.

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:04.290
But the payoff for
this is pretty big.

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:08.320
It's going to run your
tests on multiple processes.

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:12.030
So if you have
long running tests

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:14.130
this is going to make
a big improvement

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:16.800
to the total running
time of your tests.

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:18.672
Now but the downside
is like there's

00:26:18.672 --> 00:26:20.130
a little bit of
overhead associated

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:25.350
with that starting up multiple R
packages, multiple R processes.

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:27.333
Loading all the code in those.

00:26:27.333 --> 00:26:28.875
So there's a little
bit of overheads.

00:26:28.875 --> 00:26:31.800
So if your test are very fast
like if you tests all run in

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:35.260
under a second it's probably
not going to have a huge impact.

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:38.490
But if you've got tests that
take five or 10 seconds.

00:26:38.490 --> 00:26:40.830
The whole test
like takes minutes

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:43.740
then this should hopefully have
a really big positive impact

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:45.950
on your work flow.

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:51.610
There are some downsides as well
as this big upside of speed,

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.620
tests will now
kind of effectively

00:26:53.620 --> 00:26:56.590
run in stochastic
order because you'll

00:26:56.590 --> 00:26:58.640
have like four processes.

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:02.540
And each process takes kind
of the next test in the queue.

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:05.500
And so depending on exactly
how long each test takes

00:27:05.500 --> 00:27:10.120
to run like, the test might
get run in a different order.

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:12.460
And this basically
means if you have

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:15.130
any dependency
between your tests

00:27:15.130 --> 00:27:17.350
which is relatively
easy to introduce,

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:20.760
because normally they will
run in alphabetical order.

00:27:20.760 --> 00:27:23.010
If it has any dependency
between test files

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:25.180
you will get like random
test failures that

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:28.180
occur sometimes but don't
occur other times depending

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:31.750
on exactly which order
the tests are run in.

00:27:31.750 --> 00:27:36.010
In other words, this is like a
dependency debugging nightmare.

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:37.630
So we're still
thinking about tools

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:39.820
to ensure that if
that happens to you

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:43.090
we've got some debugging
mode you can switch on

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:44.320
to get more insight.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:47.620
And also if you have used like
global set up or tear down.

00:27:47.620 --> 00:27:51.790
For example, like you set up
a database or some CSV file

00:27:51.790 --> 00:27:54.520
for all of your
tests, you're going

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:57.460
to need to think that through
in a little bit more detail

00:27:57.460 --> 00:28:01.330
because those set up and
tear out files are now

00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:04.190
run by multiple processors.

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:06.880
So there's going to be a
little bit of work to convert

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:08.110
your test to use parallel.

00:28:08.110 --> 00:28:09.532
We're still working on this.

00:28:09.532 --> 00:28:11.740
There's a vignette if you
want to learn more and want

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:12.810
to try it out.

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:15.310
But we're hopeful that this
will make a big impact If you've

00:28:15.310 --> 00:28:16.268
got long running tests.

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:22.760
So I've talked about this
idea of a third edition.

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:24.500
This is a special
mode, you'll have

00:28:24.500 --> 00:28:26.990
to switch on if you want to
use all the latest and greatest

00:28:26.990 --> 00:28:27.755
testthat features.

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:34.670
We've talked about Waldo, which
makes comparisons or test makes

00:28:34.670 --> 00:28:37.820
like test failures from expect
equal and prints much, much

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:38.850
easier.

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:40.670
We talked about
snap chatting tests.

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:42.950
We talked about running
tests in parallel.

00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:44.450
Now, I just want
to show you kind

00:28:44.450 --> 00:28:48.870
of a bunch of little features
that I think are kind of cool.

00:28:48.870 --> 00:28:52.190
So in the course of working
on testthat, for this release,

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:55.040
made a bunch of improvements
to the reporters.

00:28:55.040 --> 00:29:00.500
The reporters are the things
that actually go and generate

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:01.190
the results.

00:29:01.190 --> 00:29:05.120
So when I press test this
is done by a reporter.

00:29:05.120 --> 00:29:07.960
If I press command ship t
to run all of the tests.

00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:09.770
These are different reporters.

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:13.840
One reporter that you never
normally use is called stop

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:15.615
or-- you don't normally
call it explicitly

00:29:15.615 --> 00:29:16.865
but it's called stop reporter.

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:24.990
That's the test that's run--

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:25.490
sorry.

00:29:25.490 --> 00:29:27.970
That's the report that's
run when you run a test

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:31.340
interactively so here I'm
just running a test which

00:29:31.340 --> 00:29:34.580
is not working for
probably reasons that I

00:29:34.580 --> 00:29:35.990
need to look into.

00:29:35.990 --> 00:29:37.730
But if I create--

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:40.055
up here I have another example.

00:29:40.055 --> 00:29:41.710
Over here.

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:44.990
If I run this test, this
is the stop reporter.

00:29:44.990 --> 00:29:50.930
Now, it clearly tells you that
your test passed and gives you

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:52.940
some emoji.

00:29:52.940 --> 00:30:00.120
If your test does not pass it
nicely displays the failures.

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:02.420
And it also displays
any warnings

00:30:02.420 --> 00:30:04.910
and really conveniently
it also displays

00:30:04.910 --> 00:30:06.833
the back trace for the warning.

00:30:06.833 --> 00:30:08.750
So if you've got warnings
on any of your tests

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:11.930
now they get a full back trace
so you can figure out exactly

00:30:11.930 --> 00:30:14.450
where that warning came from.

00:30:14.450 --> 00:30:19.350
One of my kind of
pet peeves is--

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:21.620
how do we do this--

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:22.655
is this I have--

00:30:27.740 --> 00:30:30.730
I have partial matching
warnings turned on.

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:32.578
And if this occurs
like somewhere deep,

00:30:32.578 --> 00:30:34.120
inside a function,
inside a function,

00:30:34.120 --> 00:30:37.430
inside another function it's
really hard to track down now

00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:38.940
on your test you
get this nice back

00:30:38.940 --> 00:30:41.790
trace so you can figure
out exactly what function,

00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:44.820
what sequence of functions
leads to that problem.

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:50.670
So that's the stop
reporter which

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:54.960
is used in debugging so now use
color, use emoji, and generally

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:57.240
it gives you more
information about problems

00:30:57.240 --> 00:30:59.700
when you're interactively
running tests.

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:07.060
I showed you earlier that
compact progress reporter that

00:31:07.060 --> 00:31:09.760
is the single line here, which
gives you kind of running this

00:31:09.760 --> 00:31:12.904
is a little bit [INAUDIBLE]
a little bit fast.

00:31:12.904 --> 00:31:26.678
So you can do the
slow test [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:26.678 --> 00:31:28.095
So just having a
little loop here,

00:31:28.095 --> 00:31:31.660
we're just going to
do an expectation

00:31:31.660 --> 00:31:34.180
and then sleep for a little bit.

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:36.920
So you can see it like count's
up now it's a little slower.

00:31:41.260 --> 00:31:46.500
So you get like a
running progress

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:48.330
bar in some sense
on all the tests

00:31:48.330 --> 00:31:50.768
that are running so you know
exactly what's going on.

00:31:55.916 --> 00:31:59.997
I've made a regular
reporter which

00:31:59.997 --> 00:32:01.580
you see most often
when you're running

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:03.470
all the tests in your package.

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:07.990
The biggest thing is I've
added a bunch of new praise.

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:16.040
That uses more emoji because
I think emoji are fun.

00:32:16.040 --> 00:32:22.400
This random praise is going to
veer a little bit into dad joke

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:23.180
territory.

00:32:23.180 --> 00:32:26.090
But hopefully, that's just
something, a fun little feature

00:32:26.090 --> 00:32:28.340
of testthat that
keeps you motivated

00:32:28.340 --> 00:32:32.370
and keeps you going when your
tests aren't working well.

00:32:32.370 --> 00:32:34.560
Finally, the last reporter
is the check reporter

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:37.230
that's run inside of
our commands check.

00:32:37.230 --> 00:32:39.460
Now it reports all
of the problems.

00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:42.420
Sort of also reports
warnings and skipped tests.

00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:44.280
It tells you all of
your skipped tests

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:47.220
by type which is really useful
with checking that you haven't

00:32:47.220 --> 00:32:49.980
accidentally skipped tests
that you meant to run

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:55.980
and it also creates a IDS
file with a machine readable

00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:57.300
list of all of the tests.

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:00.120
That's probably not something
you want to use directly

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:02.820
but it is something that we will
start to build into our tooling

00:33:02.820 --> 00:33:05.760
so that things like
get out of actions

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:11.680
can give you nice displays
of good tests and so on.

00:33:11.680 --> 00:33:13.870
We've also made a few
changes to the way

00:33:13.870 --> 00:33:19.430
that the condition
functions work.

00:33:19.430 --> 00:33:25.480
So here I have a function
that calls warn and message.

00:33:25.480 --> 00:33:31.070
And now if a condition like a
message of warning or an error

00:33:31.070 --> 00:33:33.980
is not explicitly caught
by your expectation

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:36.060
it will continue to bubble up.

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:42.070
So if I run this
line [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:42.070 --> 00:33:47.140
if I run this line this
expectation passes,

00:33:47.140 --> 00:33:49.360
it does find a
warning called high

00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:52.950
but the message called
Bye still bubbles up.

00:33:52.950 --> 00:33:56.310
Or if I say I expect
a message called Bye

00:33:56.310 --> 00:33:58.540
I'll see a warning called high.

00:33:58.540 --> 00:34:00.900
So if I want to
capture both of them

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:04.740
I need to use expect the message
and expect warning together.

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:06.840
This hopefully will
give you better control

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:08.820
over exactly what's going on.

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:12.750
It's going to cause a few
more warnings in your tests.

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:16.350
These won't cause your package
to fail our commands check

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:18.750
but they will require
a little bit of work

00:34:18.750 --> 00:34:20.940
to make sure that
those tests are

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:26.302
as you expect if you want to
just ignore them you can still

00:34:26.302 --> 00:34:28.260
rather than using
expectations and you can just

00:34:28.260 --> 00:34:35.850
switch to the base functions
suppress messages and suppress

00:34:35.850 --> 00:34:38.239
warnings if you don't
care about them.

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:44.330
Now, you might notice here that
we've got expectations next

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:46.909
to the side of expectations
and you might kind of naturally

00:34:46.909 --> 00:34:50.449
think well, why can't I
use the pipe for that,

00:34:50.449 --> 00:34:52.610
unfortunately you
cannot currently do that

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:56.659
because the pipe eagerly
evaluates everything.

00:34:56.659 --> 00:35:01.760
So if I do this you
get a Bye, and a high,

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:04.580
and then this fails
because this is

00:35:04.580 --> 00:35:07.790
called before this is called.

00:35:07.790 --> 00:35:11.810
We will start to announce
a new work in magrittr that

00:35:11.810 --> 00:35:16.130
will make this work and
hopefully make magrittr

00:35:16.130 --> 00:35:20.720
a little more compatible
with the native pipe that

00:35:20.720 --> 00:35:24.580
is unlikely to appear in the
next version of testthat.

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:32.570
So that's-- and that also means
if you're one of the 10 people

00:35:32.570 --> 00:35:35.440
in the world who used the all
the arguments-- expect warning,

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:36.380
expect message--

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:37.430
that's now deprecated.

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:39.180
You're going to take a
slightly different approach.

00:35:39.180 --> 00:35:40.555
But we're pretty
confident that's

00:35:40.555 --> 00:35:44.300
going to be a much
nicer API overall

00:35:44.300 --> 00:35:50.300
and shouldn't change
existing behavior too much.

00:35:54.180 --> 00:35:56.760
And the last thing is if
you've ever used expect error

00:35:56.760 --> 00:35:58.510
and it gives you
this message to set

00:35:58.510 --> 00:36:01.350
the class we no
longer encourage you

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:03.830
to do that it kind of
fixed one type of test

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:06.870
fidelity basically, the cost of
increasing-- just introducing

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:09.330
a different type
of fidelity and so

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:12.480
now you're better off just using
expects snapshot error if you

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:17.280
want to check that a specific
error occurs because that just

00:36:17.280 --> 00:36:18.750
gives you a bunch
of nice features

00:36:18.750 --> 00:36:20.760
for managing the
change over time.

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:26.680
Just about to wrap
up, which is great.

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:28.840
So there's plenty of
time for questions.

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:30.450
So what have I
talked about today?

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:34.470
First, testthat 3.0
is coming out soon.

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:37.140
I should say we probably
start the release process

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:39.510
in about a month, which
means that's at least

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:41.910
two months before it's on CRAN.

00:36:41.910 --> 00:36:45.390
So it would really be great
if you would try it out

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:49.530
and this is the right time to
let us know if this is causing

00:36:49.530 --> 00:36:53.280
you pain so we can fix it
before release plenty of time

00:36:53.280 --> 00:36:54.790
to do that.

00:36:54.790 --> 00:36:58.710
So the third edition is going
to be a part of testthat 3.0

00:36:58.710 --> 00:37:03.650
which will be on CRAN and
released as soon as possible

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:04.650
is probably two amounts.

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:06.690
It's likely to be
longer than that.

00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:09.270
The third addition, you will
have to deliberately opt

00:37:09.270 --> 00:37:12.660
into that gives you a
bunch of new features

00:37:12.660 --> 00:37:14.400
at the cost of doing
a little bit of work

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:17.400
to clean up old APIs.

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:21.120
The new edition uses
Waldo to make comparisons

00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:22.560
which will hopefully
make the test

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:25.230
failures much easier to debug.

00:37:25.230 --> 00:37:29.370
It provides snapshot tests which
are alternative form of testing

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:32.880
where the expected results
are stored on separate files

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:36.270
rather than in line
and code and then

00:37:36.270 --> 00:37:39.240
the testthat provides
some functions

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:42.810
to help manage these files so
that you can accept changes

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:44.940
when you have made
them deliberately

00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:48.420
or revert changes when you've
made them accidentally.

00:37:48.420 --> 00:37:53.910
Testthat 3.0 will also introduce
parallel testing, which again

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:56.080
will be a little bit
of set up work just

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:59.410
to make sure your global
setup and tear down works

00:37:59.410 --> 00:38:01.390
appropriately and
don't accidentally

00:38:01.390 --> 00:38:06.220
have any dependencies
between your tests

00:38:06.220 --> 00:38:08.200
but the payoff
will be if you have

00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:10.770
slow tests they should run
much, much faster because they

00:38:10.770 --> 00:38:11.980
run in parallel.

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:14.560
And then finally I showed
you a bunch of goodies.

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.650
Many of them featuring
emoji that will hopefully

00:38:17.650 --> 00:38:22.018
make your day to day life using
testthat a little bit more fun.

00:38:22.018 --> 00:38:23.560
So if you do want
to try it out today

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:26.590
you need to get the
development version of testthat

00:38:26.590 --> 00:38:29.670
and the development
vision of dev tools.

00:38:29.670 --> 00:38:34.400
And then in your
package, if this

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:37.900
is a package that uses
continuous integration

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:40.780
or similar you need to
add testthat to remotes

00:38:40.780 --> 00:38:43.780
and you need to accept the
addition to the third edition

00:38:43.780 --> 00:38:49.280
to take advantage of all the
latest and greatest features.

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:52.060
That was a lot of
content in 40 minutes.

00:38:52.060 --> 00:38:55.900
Again, hopefully, if you
didn't take in everything

00:38:55.900 --> 00:39:00.250
I was saying there's plenty
of additional material

00:39:00.250 --> 00:39:02.320
and all of the
vignettes, and if you

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:04.390
don't find those
understandable please

00:39:04.390 --> 00:39:07.030
let me know so we
can make them better.

00:39:07.030 --> 00:39:08.260
Thank you.

00:39:08.260 --> 00:39:10.540
And now hopefully,
Jenny, you will

00:39:10.540 --> 00:39:12.280
have some questions for me?

00:39:12.280 --> 00:39:13.270
JENNY: Yeah.

00:39:13.270 --> 00:39:15.370
So the first big one
is, I think people

00:39:15.370 --> 00:39:20.260
want to hear more about why
you're doing a third edition.

00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:24.700
So why not create an entirely
new package, the same way

00:39:24.700 --> 00:39:30.520
that plyr led to dplyr, or
why not create testthat three.

00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:34.960
Or why can you not do this
through semantic versioning

00:39:34.960 --> 00:39:37.965
of the existing package?

00:39:37.965 --> 00:39:39.840
HADLEY WICKHAM: OK, so
the first question is,

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:42.240
why not a new package?

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:44.370
And that was something
I considered.

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:49.500
But like 90% of the code
between the second edition

00:39:49.500 --> 00:39:51.370
and the third
edition are the same.

00:39:51.370 --> 00:39:54.360
The biggest difference
with the third edition

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:58.560
is it just takes stuff away from
you, stuff that we now regret.

00:39:58.560 --> 00:40:01.290
So if we created a
new package, we'd

00:40:01.290 --> 00:40:05.225
have two packages that have
like 90% overlap in the code,

00:40:05.225 --> 00:40:06.600
and whenever there
was a bug we'd

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:09.610
have to remember to fix
them in both packages.

00:40:09.610 --> 00:40:13.060
So I think for this case,
most of the stuff is the same,

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:16.050
we're just trying to get
rid of some new things.

00:40:18.910 --> 00:40:20.970
I think creating a new
package would be overkill.

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:26.730
The question about
semantic visioning

00:40:26.730 --> 00:40:29.610
is something that, yes, that
does use semantic versioning,

00:40:29.610 --> 00:40:32.350
this is a major vision of
testthat three point 0.

00:40:32.350 --> 00:40:36.360
But that actually doesn't
really help in the R community,

00:40:36.360 --> 00:40:41.010
because you only have a single
version of a package installed

00:40:41.010 --> 00:40:42.270
on your computer.

00:40:42.270 --> 00:40:44.740
In a library you can
kind of work around that,

00:40:44.740 --> 00:40:46.170
but it's a little fiddly.

00:40:46.170 --> 00:40:49.110
And if a package is
on CRAN they always

00:40:49.110 --> 00:40:52.140
use the latest version
of the package on CRAN.

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:55.330
So if I just released
testthat three point 0,

00:40:55.330 --> 00:40:57.930
with all of these
new features on it,

00:40:57.930 --> 00:41:00.060
something like
500 packages would

00:41:00.060 --> 00:41:02.250
break on CRAN, because
they would automatically

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:05.100
use the version of
testthat that on CRAN.

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:08.610
So I think that
that's why we want

00:41:08.610 --> 00:41:11.190
to make a little bit of
friction so that you have

00:41:11.190 --> 00:41:14.860
to deliberately choose
to use this new testing,

00:41:14.860 --> 00:41:17.640
use these new
conventions, basically

00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:20.070
stop using old stuff that
you should have stopped using

00:41:20.070 --> 00:41:25.050
a while ago without
duplicating a bunch of code

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:29.230
that we didn't have to
maintain in two places.

00:41:29.230 --> 00:41:32.660
JENNY: OK, so now I have
a series of questions,

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:35.520
and the list is growing
longer, that are smaller,

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:38.070
and we'll just work
through as many as we can.

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:43.250
So this first one is, have you
tested whether snapshotting

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:47.840
plays nice with things like
cover or the good practice

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:49.160
package?

00:41:49.160 --> 00:41:53.570
Does this third edition work
have any effect on shinytest?

00:41:53.570 --> 00:41:58.200
So talking about how this
works with other packages.

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:00.680
HADLEY WICKHAM: Yeah,
so snapshot testing

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:03.980
is just testing, it just
works exactly the way

00:42:03.980 --> 00:42:05.810
you'd expect with cover.

00:42:05.810 --> 00:42:09.020
I don't know that much
about good practice.

00:42:09.020 --> 00:42:11.300
I would think it
would not, I don't

00:42:11.300 --> 00:42:15.890
think it would cause
problems for good practice.

00:42:15.890 --> 00:42:21.040
shinytest-- it's not
clear precisely how this

00:42:21.040 --> 00:42:23.380
will play out.

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:27.220
I've been having bigger
discussions with the shiny team

00:42:27.220 --> 00:42:32.130
about how testing
shiny should work,

00:42:32.130 --> 00:42:34.270
because my vision
for testing shiny

00:42:34.270 --> 00:42:37.950
is quite different from their
vision of testing shiny apps.

00:42:37.950 --> 00:42:39.990
So we're just
working towards that.

00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:42.930
I think, in an ideal
world, eventually

00:42:42.930 --> 00:42:46.710
shiny tests would use more
of this new snapshotting

00:42:46.710 --> 00:42:50.910
infrastructure that
testthat provides.

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:52.380
But there's some
other big changes

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:53.820
that have to happen
to shinytest,

00:42:53.820 --> 00:42:58.140
so it's hard to say today
whether that shinytest will

00:42:58.140 --> 00:43:01.600
eventually integrate
this better or there

00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:03.810
will be a new package
that's a successor

00:43:03.810 --> 00:43:07.830
to shinytest that
uses some other stuff

00:43:07.830 --> 00:43:11.820
and is more focused on
snapshot testing than testthat.

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:16.560
If you want to see my
take on testing shiny,

00:43:16.560 --> 00:43:20.070
I'm working on this
chapter at the moment.

00:43:20.070 --> 00:43:22.300
If you follow the
shiny test repo,

00:43:22.300 --> 00:43:26.040
you'll notice that
I've recently triaged

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:28.050
like 100 and something issues.

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:30.150
So I'm thinking
about this a lot,

00:43:30.150 --> 00:43:31.830
I'm starting to work on it.

00:43:31.830 --> 00:43:33.020
I'm thinking about the test.

00:43:33.020 --> 00:43:35.570
I don't exactly know
what the answer is,

00:43:35.570 --> 00:43:38.580
but you can see
my latest thinking

00:43:38.580 --> 00:43:41.490
in this package and this
chapter of mastering

00:43:41.490 --> 00:43:46.140
shiny which will get
updated pretty rapidly

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:49.300
in the next week or two.

00:43:49.300 --> 00:43:52.090
JENNY: All right, the next
one is, do test snapshots

00:43:52.090 --> 00:43:55.300
need to be included in
the package or could

00:43:55.300 --> 00:43:57.070
you exclude them
if they're large

00:43:57.070 --> 00:44:01.890
and then developers can recreate
them from a stable version?

00:44:01.890 --> 00:44:03.640
HADLEY WICKHAM: I think
if you want people

00:44:03.640 --> 00:44:04.990
to be able to run
your tests, you'd

00:44:04.990 --> 00:44:06.532
have to include them
in your package.

00:44:10.230 --> 00:44:14.700
Because they are literally
like the correct output.

00:44:14.700 --> 00:44:16.620
One thing I did
forget to mention

00:44:16.620 --> 00:44:18.570
is that snapshot tests
are not [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:18.570 --> 00:44:20.790
will be skipped on
CRAN automatically.

00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:23.280
You can opt out of that with
an argument if you want.

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:25.820
Just because if a
snapshot test fails,

00:44:25.820 --> 00:44:28.710
it isn't necessarily
true that it's

00:44:28.710 --> 00:44:30.303
like a real, meaningful failure.

00:44:30.303 --> 00:44:32.470
It might be an incidental
difference or a difference

00:44:32.470 --> 00:44:34.387
from a downstream package
which you don't want

00:44:34.387 --> 00:44:38.030
to cause a failure on CRAN.

00:44:38.030 --> 00:44:41.220
But yes, if you want
people to run your tests

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:44.515
you have to include the
expected result in the package.

00:44:44.515 --> 00:44:48.020
JENNY: All right, is it
possible to use snapshot tests

00:44:48.020 --> 00:44:51.020
if the function does
not return anything

00:44:51.020 --> 00:44:54.590
but rather has a side effect?

00:44:54.590 --> 00:44:59.340
HADLEY WICKHAM: So
I mentioned briefly

00:44:59.340 --> 00:45:02.400
expect snapshot which captures
the side effects of messages,

00:45:02.400 --> 00:45:04.560
warnings, and errors.

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:08.640
If it's making other
changes to the global state,

00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:12.140
you would need to make
that kind of explicit.

00:45:12.140 --> 00:45:16.680
Like if your function
attaches a package,

00:45:16.680 --> 00:45:26.290
you might expect snapshots
like search, which

00:45:26.290 --> 00:45:28.470
would capture the search path.

00:45:28.470 --> 00:45:31.000
Or maybe if it's creating
files in the directory

00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:33.400
you could use--

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:37.630
so it doesn't directly
handle side effects,

00:45:37.630 --> 00:45:39.820
apart from messages,
warnings and errors.

00:45:39.820 --> 00:45:43.060
But you could easily wrap
up a little function that

00:45:43.060 --> 00:45:45.920
captures those side effects
and makes them explicit

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:46.830
and snapshot that.

00:45:49.450 --> 00:45:51.910
JENNY: All right, the next
person missed a little bit

00:45:51.910 --> 00:45:54.040
about what's going
on with context

00:45:54.040 --> 00:45:59.620
but seems that it's deprecated
and thinks that it's

00:45:59.620 --> 00:46:02.500
needed when the JUnit reporter.

00:46:02.500 --> 00:46:06.118
So how are those two
things going to work out?

00:46:06.118 --> 00:46:07.160
HADLEY WICKHAM: I don't--

00:46:07.160 --> 00:46:12.450
so let me just
quickly find a package

00:46:12.450 --> 00:46:16.970
just so I can explain the
contexts for this change

00:46:16.970 --> 00:46:20.100
the context a little more.

00:46:20.100 --> 00:46:21.820
OK, so this is just
the per package.

00:46:21.820 --> 00:46:24.820
I just searched to find
all the uses of context.

00:46:24.820 --> 00:46:29.920
And you can see like
the test-lmap.r contains

00:46:29.920 --> 00:46:32.005
what's called a context L map.

00:46:32.005 --> 00:46:39.070
The test as-mapper.r, as it
contains a call to context

00:46:39.070 --> 00:46:43.330
as _mapper, which is a
little mildly inconsistent.

00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:47.470
Or test-win contains
a call to context-win.

00:46:47.470 --> 00:46:50.140
test-utils contains a
call to context utils.

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:52.690
So by and large, it's
like a one to one

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:54.970
mapping between what
you put in the context

00:46:54.970 --> 00:46:56.440
and the name of the file.

00:46:56.440 --> 00:47:01.960
And that is just like, you
don't want that duplication,

00:47:01.960 --> 00:47:04.450
because you end up
with inconsistencies.

00:47:04.450 --> 00:47:07.970
Or maybe, if I
can find here, you

00:47:07.970 --> 00:47:12.470
can see that this is
a test for P mapper.

00:47:12.470 --> 00:47:14.310
It probably used
to be test-pmapper.

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:18.140
We renamed the file and forgot
to update the test context.

00:47:18.140 --> 00:47:21.080
And so now there's like this
mismatch between the two.

00:47:21.080 --> 00:47:23.570
So that's the reason why
we're getting rid of context,

00:47:23.570 --> 00:47:29.450
because it just introduces this
duplication for no real gains.

00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:31.820
But the context
is still implicit,

00:47:31.820 --> 00:47:35.340
we just take the file
names of the context that

00:47:35.340 --> 00:47:37.850
tests the JUnit reporter,
for example, we'll

00:47:37.850 --> 00:47:42.140
use will just be map_in, so
it shouldn't change anything

00:47:42.140 --> 00:47:44.770
practically with
the JUnit reporter.

00:47:47.570 --> 00:47:50.630
JENNY: OK, so this is
a person like me who

00:47:50.630 --> 00:47:53.810
uses with mock and local
mock, so if they're

00:47:53.810 --> 00:47:56.210
going to be deprecated,
what is the suggested way

00:47:56.210 --> 00:48:00.350
to mock functions in
external packages?

00:48:00.350 --> 00:48:04.460
I read that the goal of
mocker, package mocker,

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:06.350
is to provide a
drop in replacement,

00:48:06.350 --> 00:48:08.000
but it does not
have this feature.

00:48:11.540 --> 00:48:18.290
HADLEY WICKHAM: So basically,
the way that with mock works

00:48:18.290 --> 00:48:26.450
is truly an abuse of
R, and does something

00:48:26.450 --> 00:48:30.130
that I now believe to be
tremendously ill-advised,

00:48:30.130 --> 00:48:33.485
and the chances of it stopping
working in a future vision of R

00:48:33.485 --> 00:48:34.630
are high.

00:48:34.630 --> 00:48:38.845
Because what it does is
so bizarre and horrific.

00:48:41.350 --> 00:48:45.010
So basically you have
to use another approach.

00:48:45.010 --> 00:48:49.030
So both the mocker
and mockery packages

00:48:49.030 --> 00:48:51.520
provide slightly
different techniques.

00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:55.710
Unfortunately, you just have
to you have to use those.

00:48:55.710 --> 00:49:01.960
I'm just so concerned that
the way that with mock works

00:49:01.960 --> 00:49:06.810
is not good, and it's
surprising that it hasn't

00:49:06.810 --> 00:49:08.560
caused any problems to date.

00:49:08.560 --> 00:49:12.630
So unfortunately,
it's something that

00:49:12.630 --> 00:49:15.250
is really nice and really
useful but is achieved

00:49:15.250 --> 00:49:19.330
and the really,
really horrible way.

00:49:19.330 --> 00:49:24.890
JENNY: Does snapshot have a
limit on the size of the data,

00:49:24.890 --> 00:49:29.970
and can you combine snapshot
tests with more explicit tests

00:49:29.970 --> 00:49:32.900
of the snapshot?

00:49:32.900 --> 00:49:37.450
HADLEY WICKHAM: Yeah, so
there's no limit on the size.

00:49:37.450 --> 00:49:44.610
And I think, generally, you
will mingle snapshot testing

00:49:44.610 --> 00:49:45.570
with regular testing.

00:49:45.570 --> 00:49:47.670
Like where you can,
like this was a list,

00:49:47.670 --> 00:49:50.540
you might want to test that
his is a specific value.

00:49:50.540 --> 00:49:53.700
And there's another
component of the list

00:49:53.700 --> 00:50:00.280
that's just a big blob that
you don't want to type out.

00:50:00.280 --> 00:50:03.420
So I really imagine that you
would want to mingle those

00:50:03.420 --> 00:50:05.640
as much as possible.

00:50:05.640 --> 00:50:10.287
I think it will be tempting
to overuse snapshot testing

00:50:10.287 --> 00:50:12.120
just because it's so
convenient, because you

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:15.338
don't have to write the right
hand side of expect equal.

00:50:15.338 --> 00:50:17.880
You don't have to make clear in
your head exactly what you're

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:22.288
expecting, but I think if you
do that, if you use snapshotting

00:50:22.288 --> 00:50:24.330
too much, you'll find your
tests are a little bit

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:28.320
fragile and kind of break more
often than you'd otherwise

00:50:28.320 --> 00:50:30.480
like, and that will push
you, naturally, back

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:34.140
towards the finer grains
unit testing that you're

00:50:34.140 --> 00:50:37.352
used to in testthat.

00:50:37.352 --> 00:50:39.560
Of course, putting really
large files in your package

00:50:39.560 --> 00:50:42.370
is going to be annoying
for other reasons.

00:50:42.370 --> 00:50:46.430
So I don't think you want to put
megabytes of data using this,

00:50:46.430 --> 00:50:49.580
but certainly like kilobytes
of data, which would be a pain

00:50:49.580 --> 00:50:51.520
to put in a test file.

00:50:51.520 --> 00:50:56.380
It's a really nice, sweet
spot for snapshot testing.

00:50:56.380 --> 00:50:58.120
JENNY: OK, I have
two questions that

00:50:58.120 --> 00:51:01.600
are a little bit
of emoji push-back.

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:04.720
Not to be a killjoy, but
praise seems unnecessary.

00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:07.750
Why not include it as
a suggested package?

00:51:07.750 --> 00:51:11.740
And then also, is there a way
to suppress emojis or build

00:51:11.740 --> 00:51:15.710
pipelines that may
not support them?

00:51:15.710 --> 00:51:17.890
HADLEY WICKHAM: So
some people really--

00:51:17.890 --> 00:51:20.470
so first of all, it doesn't
use the praise package.

00:51:20.470 --> 00:51:23.340
It just uses its own.

00:51:23.340 --> 00:51:25.910
I guess it does, is that right?

00:51:25.910 --> 00:51:28.540
Let me refresh my memory.

00:51:28.540 --> 00:51:30.550
I guess it does use the
praise package which

00:51:30.550 --> 00:51:33.880
is a tiny package, so
I guess we could make

00:51:33.880 --> 00:51:38.340
this a suggested package, but it
just seems like, I don't know.

00:51:38.340 --> 00:51:40.660
If someone did a pull
request that made praise

00:51:40.660 --> 00:51:43.430
into a suggested package rather
than a dependent package,

00:51:43.430 --> 00:51:44.240
I'd consider it.

00:51:44.240 --> 00:51:49.710
But I don't think it
practically really reduces

00:51:49.710 --> 00:51:53.400
the cost of installing testthat
that in a practical way.

00:51:53.400 --> 00:51:58.160
If you don't want emojis, you
can't turn the emojis off,

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:00.720
or they will be
automatically turned off

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:05.850
if you're not in a UTF 8 locale
which is normally on Windows.

00:52:05.850 --> 00:52:09.905
But you can-- test, report--

00:52:14.362 --> 00:52:15.820
I can't remember,
what's it called?

00:52:15.820 --> 00:52:17.015
Progress report.

00:52:17.015 --> 00:52:18.390
If you really
don't like them you

00:52:18.390 --> 00:52:22.795
can turn them off by setting
stuff up in a special way

00:52:22.795 --> 00:52:25.330
and and saying show
praise equals false.

00:52:25.330 --> 00:52:28.540
But the praise only
shows up in reporters

00:52:28.540 --> 00:52:30.070
that are used interactively.

00:52:30.070 --> 00:52:34.780
It never shows up
in reporters that

00:52:34.780 --> 00:52:39.040
are used in R commands check
or other continuous integration

00:52:39.040 --> 00:52:39.830
context.

00:52:39.830 --> 00:52:43.942
So I it's purely
just a fun thing,

00:52:43.942 --> 00:52:46.150
I don't think there's anything
wrong with introducing

00:52:46.150 --> 00:52:49.660
a little fun and joy
into unit testing,

00:52:49.660 --> 00:52:52.270
and by and large,
people really like it.

00:52:52.270 --> 00:52:55.910
But if you don't,
you can turn it off.

00:52:55.910 --> 00:52:58.470
JENNY: OK, I've been
using a Waldo a bit.

00:52:58.470 --> 00:53:02.090
Is there any planned way to
control the amount of output

00:53:02.090 --> 00:53:02.870
that is shown?

00:53:06.227 --> 00:53:07.060
HADLEY WICKHAM: Yes.

00:53:13.110 --> 00:53:18.840
So someone actually just
had a poll request for Waldo

00:53:18.840 --> 00:53:22.170
so that you can choose to
print any number of changes.

00:53:22.170 --> 00:53:24.150
By default, Waldo
doesn't show-- it

00:53:24.150 --> 00:53:27.690
shows like 10 changes
with the assumption

00:53:27.690 --> 00:53:31.410
that if you have
more than 10 changes,

00:53:31.410 --> 00:53:35.550
you probably want to start by
fixing the first 10 changes,

00:53:35.550 --> 00:53:39.660
and that will
reveal more issues.

00:53:39.660 --> 00:53:41.840
I think this is something--

00:53:41.840 --> 00:53:45.450
this is just purely
driven by pragmatics.

00:53:45.450 --> 00:53:48.690
You don't want failures is
that generate hundreds of lines

00:53:48.690 --> 00:53:52.140
of output wind there's
something horrifically

00:53:52.140 --> 00:53:56.020
wrong in the first line
that is actionable.

00:53:56.020 --> 00:53:59.970
So I don't have a good sense
of where that slider should

00:53:59.970 --> 00:54:02.100
be set by default and
how you should slide it

00:54:02.100 --> 00:54:06.060
to more or less output
yet, but that's something

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:08.380
that we'll keep thinking
about and iterating on

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:14.310
as we work on this in
reality and our packages.

00:54:14.310 --> 00:54:16.300
JENNY: I've picked
two last questions,

00:54:16.300 --> 00:54:18.720
one is super tiny then
one's a little bit bigger

00:54:18.720 --> 00:54:21.040
and makes a good conclusion.

00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:22.950
So the tiny, off
topic question is,

00:54:22.950 --> 00:54:26.677
how do you get the git branch
into your prompt in the R

00:54:26.677 --> 00:54:28.682
console?

00:54:28.682 --> 00:54:31.140
HADLEY WICKHAM: So if you're
wondering what this thing was,

00:54:31.140 --> 00:54:35.040
this is the name of
my active git branch,

00:54:35.040 --> 00:54:39.600
and that is some
code on my R profile

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:43.140
that uses [INAUDIBLE]
prompt package.

00:54:43.140 --> 00:54:45.840
Just pop the git branch--

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:49.740
and then this
automatically updates

00:54:49.740 --> 00:54:52.770
whenever you run in the
archive, so it automatically

00:54:52.770 --> 00:54:56.083
stays up to date as
you change the branch.

00:54:56.083 --> 00:54:57.500
JENNY: OK, so the
last question, I

00:54:57.500 --> 00:54:59.090
think it's a good
one to close on.

00:54:59.090 --> 00:55:02.810
Is to think about,
what's the long term game

00:55:02.810 --> 00:55:04.670
plan with this addition idea?

00:55:04.670 --> 00:55:08.900
So this person is like, for
now in the dev version, after I

00:55:08.900 --> 00:55:13.460
install it, I have to
change this config entry

00:55:13.460 --> 00:55:15.170
and description.

00:55:15.170 --> 00:55:18.200
Will this still be
the case indefinitely

00:55:18.200 --> 00:55:20.840
when it's not the dev
version but it's the released

00:55:20.840 --> 00:55:25.490
version, or at some point will
it default to edition three

00:55:25.490 --> 00:55:30.112
and someone might have
to opt into edition two?

00:55:30.112 --> 00:55:31.570
HADLEY WICKHAM: I
don't see any way

00:55:31.570 --> 00:55:35.320
to change the default additions
from two without breaking

00:55:35.320 --> 00:55:37.370
hundreds of CRAN packages.

00:55:37.370 --> 00:55:40.090
So I think that implies that
the default version will always

00:55:40.090 --> 00:55:41.680
be two.

00:55:41.680 --> 00:55:44.620
But what we will do
in the tools that we

00:55:44.620 --> 00:55:47.170
use for creating packages,
like in use this,

00:55:47.170 --> 00:55:50.680
we will automatically set the
conflict to the latest version.

00:55:50.680 --> 00:55:53.290
So when you start
the idea is that when

00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:58.750
you start a new package you get
the latest edition of testthat.

00:55:58.750 --> 00:56:00.850
When you are working
on an old package,

00:56:00.850 --> 00:56:03.250
you have to deliberately
opt into the new version

00:56:03.250 --> 00:56:04.570
if you really want it.

00:56:04.570 --> 00:56:08.520
I think that offers the
best compromise of packages

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:10.120
that actively develop
and can easily

00:56:10.120 --> 00:56:12.610
upgrade without
touching anything

00:56:12.610 --> 00:56:14.946
that's not being developed.

00:56:14.946 --> 00:56:20.060
Thanks for joining us and
learning about the upcoming

00:56:20.060 --> 00:56:23.270
third edition of testthat.

00:56:23.270 --> 00:56:27.320
Now is the perfect time
to get feedback on things

00:56:27.320 --> 00:56:29.890
you like, things that you hate.

00:56:29.890 --> 00:56:34.180
We're listening, and we really
appreciate your feedback.

